A stack is a data structure that works on the principle of Last In First Out (LIFO). It is used to store and retrieve elements in a particular order. The basic operations of a stack are push, pop, peek, and isEmpty.

1. Push: This operation adds an element to the top of the stack and increases the stack size by one.
2. Pop: This operation removes the element from the top of the stack and decreases the stack size by one.
3. Peek: This operation retrieves the element at the top of the stack without removing it.
4. IsEmpty: This operation checks if the stack is empty or not.

Here is an example of a stack implemented in Java:
```java
public class Stack {
    private int[] stackArray;
    private int top;

    public Stack(int capacity) {
        stackArray = new int[capacity];
        top = -1;
    }

    public void push(int item) {
        stackArray[++top] = item;
    }

    public int pop() {
        return stackArray[top--];
    }

    public int peek() {
        return stackArray[top];
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return (top == -1);
    }
}
```
A queue is a data structure that works on the principle of First In First Out (FIFO). It is used to store and retrieve elements in a particular order. The basic operations of a queue are enqueue, dequeue, peek, and isEmpty.

1. Enqueue: This operation adds an element to the rear of the queue and increases the queue size by one.
2. Dequeue: This operation removes the element from the front of the queue and decreases the queue size by one.
3. Peek: This operation retrieves the element at the front of the queue without removing it.
4. IsEmpty: This operation checks if the queue is empty or not.

Here is an example of a queue implemented in Java:
```java
public class Queue {
    private int[] queueArray;
    private int rear;
    private int front;
    private int size;

    public Queue(int capacity) {
        queueArray = new int[capacity];
        rear = -1;
        front = -1;
        size = 0;
    }

    public void enqueue(int item) {
        if (size == queueArray.length) {
            return;
        }
        rear++;
        queueArray[rear] = item;
        size++;
    }

    public int dequeue() {
        if (size == 0) {
            return -1;
        }
        front++;
        int item = queueArray[front];
        size--;
        return item;
    }

    public int peek() {
        if (size == 0) {
            return -1;
        }
        return queueArray[front];
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return (size == 0);
    }
}
```
Note: The above implementation of the queue is a basic example and does not handle the case when the queue is full or when the queue is empty.

