It looks like you have implemented a partition function for quicksort, which is a good start. However, you also need to implement the quicksort function itself, which will call the partition function to divide the array into two parts, and then recursively sort each part.

Here is an example implementation of quicksort that meets the requirements you specified:
```
void quicksort(int nums[], int low, int high) {
    if (low < high) {
        int pivot_index = partition(nums, low, high);
        quicksort(nums, low, pivot_index - 1);
        quicksort(nums, pivot_index + 1, high);
    }
}

int partition(int nums[], int low, int high) {
    int pivot = nums[high];
    int i = low - 1;

    for (int j = low; j <= high - 1; j++) {
        if (nums[j] <= pivot) {
            i++;
            swap(nums[i], nums[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(nums[i + 1], nums[high]);
    return (i + 1);
}
```
This implementation uses a pivot selection algorithm called "median of three", which is a simple and efficient way to select a pivot element. The partition function uses a swap-based approach to move elements around until the pivot element is in its final position. The quicksort function then recursively sorts the two sub-arrays created by the partition function.

